<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/12th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jin–Song wars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1125">1125</a>–<a href="/years/1234">1234</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jin–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> wars were a series of conflicts fought from 1125 to 1234 between the Jurchen-led Jin dynasty and the Song dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jingkang-incident">Jingkang incident</a> of 1127 the Jin took the Song capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> and captured two emperors, forcing the Song court south to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hangzhou">Hangzhou</a>. Generals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yue-fei">Yue Fei</a> resisted, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shaoxing">Treaty of Shaoxing</a> (1142) fixed the border on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huai-river">Huai River</a>; later wars changed little before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> destroyed the Jin in 1234.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jin-song-wars.jpg" alt="Jin–Song wars" width="320" height="309" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ian Kiu · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jin_Dynasty_1141_(no_borders).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The two powers began as allies. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-jin">Wanyan Aguda</a> united the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a> tribes and in 1115 named himself emperor of the Jin, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-huizong">Huizong</a> sought his help against the Khitan-led Liao dynasty, hoping to recover the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-prefectures">Sixteen Prefectures</a> lost in 938. The Jin took the Liao capitals quickly, while Song attacks on Yanjing failed twice in 1122. By the treaty of 1123 the Song received only seven prefectures and agreed to pay the Jin 300,000 bolts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and 200,000 taels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> a year.</p><p>The alliance soon broke down. Zhang Jue, a former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> governor, turned his city over to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, though the treaty forbade harbouring defectors, and in 1124 Song officials asked for nine more prefectures. In November 1125 the Jin emperor Taizong ordered the attack.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Two Jin armies invaded. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> was first besieged on 31 January 1126, and after 33 days the new emperor Qinzong bought a withdrawal by ceding three prefectures; when he went back on the agreement the Jin returned and broke into the city on 9 January 1127. The Jin then tried to rule the north through puppet states, Da Chu in 1127 and Da Qi from 1130 to 1137. Gaozong, enthroned on 12 June 1127, was pursued as far as the sea in 1130, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> navy on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze</a> and an insurgency in the north checked the invaders.</p><p>The peace of 1142 was broken twice. The Jin emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wanyan-liang">Wanyan Liang</a> invaded in 1161, was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-caishi">Battle of Caishi</a> in November and was assassinated by his own officers in December. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-tuozhou">Han Tuozhou</a> war was declared on 14 June 1206; the offensive failed, and the treaty of 2 November 1208 raised the tribute. Pressed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a>, the Jin attacked the Song again from 1217 until a peace in 1224 ended the tribute. In 1233 the Song allied with the Mongols, and in 1234 their troops took Caizhou, where the Jin emperor Aizong killed himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Some two to three million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchens</a> came to rule about 30 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> in the north. Over half a million people fled south in 1126–1127. Lin'an, declared the capital in 1138, grew into a major commercial centre. The wars also spurred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> weapons: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fire-lance">fire lance</a> is first reported at the siege of De'an in 1132, and the iron-cased bombs the Jin used at Qizhou in 1221 are the first known hard-casing bombs. After the Jin fell the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> turned on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>, which collapsed in 1279.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jin–Song wars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
